WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.780
Two species eat the same limited food, and one grows twice as fast.

2
00:00:09.260 --> 00:00:10.400
Must the faster one win?

3
00:00:11.620 --> 00:00:13.540
This film builds the test that decides.

4
00:00:14.683 --> 00:00:16.563
Start with the teal species alone.

5
00:00:17.943 --> 00:00:23.343
Its numbers rise toward its carrying capacity, Big K, the size it settles at alone.

6
00:00:24.783 --> 00:00:27.783
Picture that capacity as a bar of room that fills up.

7
00:00:28.733 --> 00:00:31.633
As the room fills, growth per individual slows.

8
00:00:32.993 --> 00:00:37.233
That is the per-capita growth rate, and it reaches zero when the bar is full.

9
00:00:38.317 --> 00:00:39.677
Now bring back orange.

10
00:00:41.117 --> 00:00:43.717
Each orange individual takes some of teal's room too.

11
00:00:45.037 --> 00:00:48.737
A number called alpha says how much, counted in teal individuals.

12
00:00:49.900 --> 00:00:53.120
Read the two numbers after alpha as on, then from.

13
00:00:54.460 --> 00:00:58.160
alpha12 is the effect on species 1, from species 2.

14
00:00:59.400 --> 00:01:02.240
Orange has its own room bar, filled the same way.

15
00:01:03.733 --> 00:01:05.973
Now plot both species at once.

16
00:01:07.193 --> 00:01:10.033
Teal numbers run across, and orange numbers run up.

17
00:01:11.173 --> 00:01:14.153
The teal line marks every mix where teal stops growing.

18
00:01:15.433 --> 00:01:18.073
On one side teal grows; on the other it shrinks.

19
00:01:19.333 --> 00:01:22.753
That line is teal's isocline, its zero-growth line.

20
00:01:24.193 --> 00:01:25.213
Orange gets one too.

21
00:01:26.433 --> 00:01:29.573
Where the two isoclines cross, neither species changes.

22
00:01:30.933 --> 00:01:32.633
Is that crossing where they end up?

23
00:01:33.567 --> 00:01:39.727
Here is the test. Shrink teal to a few individuals, and let orange sit at its own

24
00:01:39.727 --> 00:01:40.847
carrying capacity.

25
00:01:42.367 --> 00:01:45.847
Can teal grow? This is the invasion test.

26
00:01:46.867 --> 00:01:50.687
Here teal's arrow points away from zero, so rare teal grows.

27
00:01:52.287 --> 00:01:54.207
Swap roles, and rare orange grows too.

28
00:01:55.687 --> 00:02:01.607
Both pass. Every path that starts with both species present flows into the crossing.

29
00:02:02.867 --> 00:02:04.067
They coexist stably.

30
00:02:05.200 --> 00:02:08.860
Now make teal hurt orange more, so orange's line tilts.

31
00:02:10.220 --> 00:02:13.240
Rare orange, among a full teal population, now shrinks.

32
00:02:14.560 --> 00:02:17.760
Teal passes, orange fails, and orange slides to zero.

33
00:02:19.160 --> 00:02:20.640
Teal excludes it from this place.

34
00:02:21.700 --> 00:02:24.560
Cross the lines the other way, and neither passes.

35
00:02:26.000 --> 00:02:29.880
Each species wins from some starts, so the starting numbers decide.

36
00:02:30.983 --> 00:02:32.743
Back to the pair that coexist.

37
00:02:34.103 --> 00:02:37.323
Now double orange's growth rate, and change nothing else.

38
00:02:38.583 --> 00:02:39.663
Does the ending change?

39
00:02:46.200 --> 00:02:47.700
The ending stays put.

40
00:02:48.900 --> 00:02:53.700
Orange moves faster and the paths bend, but they still finish at the same crossing.

41
00:02:55.080 --> 00:02:57.800
Here, growth rate changes the route, not the outcome.

42
00:02:59.040 --> 00:03:01.820
So in this model, the faster grower need not win.

43
00:03:02.883 --> 00:03:07.123
In words, each species must limit itself more than it limits the other.

44
00:03:08.703 --> 00:03:11.783
Limits means the share of each room that one individual fills.

45
00:03:12.933 --> 00:03:16.793
So the whole verdict comes from one question, asked of each species.

46
00:03:18.273 --> 00:03:21.293
Can it grow when rare, beside a competitor at full strength?
